Let $Q(t)$ be the amount of salt (in oz) in the tank at time $t$ (in minutes).
The volume of the solution in the tank remains constant at $V = 100$ gallons because the inflow rate ($2 \text{ gal/min}$) equals the outflow rate ($2 \text{ gal/min}$).

The rate of change of salt in the tank is given by:
$$\frac{dQ}{dt} = (\text{Rate of salt in}) - (\text{Rate of salt out})$$

Rate of salt in:
$$\text{Rate in} = (\text{Concentration in}) \times (\text{Flow rate in}) = \frac{1}{4}\left(1+\frac{1}{2} \sin t\right) \frac{\text{oz}}{\text{gal}} \times 2 \frac{\text{gal}}{\text{min}} = \frac{1}{2}\left(1+\frac{1}{2} \sin t\right) = \frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{4} \sin t$$

Rate of salt out:
The concentration in the tank at time $t$ is $\frac{Q(t)}{V} = \frac{Q(t)}{100} \frac{\text{oz}}{\text{gal}}$.
$$\text{Rate out} = (\text{Concentration out}) \times (\text{Flow rate out}) = \frac{Q(t)}{100} \times 2 = \frac{Q(t)}{50}$$

The differential equation is:
$$\frac{dQ}{dt} = \frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{4} \sin t - \frac{Q}{50}$$
$$\frac{dQ}{dt} + \frac{1}{50} Q = \frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{4} \sin t$$

This is a first-order linear differential equation. The long-time behavior is the particular solution, which will be a constant plus a sinusoidal term.
The general solution is $Q(t) = Q_h(t) + Q_p(t)$.
The homogeneous solution is $Q_h(t) = C e^{-t/50}$. As $t \to \infty$, $Q_h(t) \to 0$.

The particular solution $Q_p(t)$ will have the form $Q_p(t) = A + B \cos t + C \sin t$.
Substituting into the ODE:
$$(-B \sin t + C \cos t) + \frac{1}{50}(A + B \cos t + C \sin t) = \frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{4} \sin t$$
Grouping terms:
$$A \left(\frac{1}{50}\right) + \left(B \frac{1}{50} + C \frac{1}{50}\right) \cos t + \left(C \frac{1}{50} - B \frac{1}{50}\right) \sin t = \frac{1}{2} + 0 \cos t + \frac{1}{4} \sin t$$

Comparing coefficients:
1. Constant term: $\frac{A}{50} = \frac{1}{2} \implies A = 25$.
2. $\cos t$ term: $\frac{B}{50} = 0 \implies B = 0$.
3. $\sin t$ term: $\frac{C}{50} - \frac{B}{50} = \frac{1}{4}$. Since $B=0$, $\frac{C}{50} = \frac{1}{4} \implies C = \frac{50}{4} = \frac{25}{2} = 12.5$.

So the particular solution is $Q_p(t) = 25 + 12.5 \sin t$.
The long-time behavior is $Q(t) \approx 25 + 12.5 \sin t$.
The oscillation is about the constant level $25$.
The amplitude of the oscillation is $|12.5|$.

